WEBVTT
TITLE Mathematical Induction — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Forty dominoes, one push. You do not push forty times: you push once and check that each one topples the next.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
The statements here are infinitely many: P of one, P of two, P of three, with no last one to check.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
So the work moves into one link: from a statement about n to the same statement about n plus one.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Base plus link gives every statement, and neither alone gives any. That is the whole method.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
The rule in symbols: P of zero, and P of n implies P of n plus one for every n, therefore P of m for every m.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Unfolded, the link is a chain: P zero gives P one, which gives P two, which gives P three, and so on.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
A written proof has five parts: name the method, define P of n, prove the base, prove the step, invoke the rule.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
The hypothesis is not circular: you assume P of n to prove P of n plus one, which proves the implication, not P itself.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Claim: one cubed plus two cubed up to n cubed equals the square of one plus two up to n.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Base case, n equals one: one cubed is one, and the square of one is one. The two sides agree.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Step: the n plus one case adds n plus one cubed to the hypothesis, and the right side grows by exactly the same amount.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
The page checks n from one to three hundred and the residual is zero every time, but the check is evidence, not the proof.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Claim: two to the n beats n squared. At n equal to one it is true, and at n equal to two it is already false.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
So the claim is not true for all n. It is true for n at least five, and the base case has to be five, not one.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
The step needs n plus one squared below two n squared, which holds from n equal to three: sixteen is below eighteen.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The base must sit inside the step domain and the chain must cover the claim. Base one with step three covers only n equal to one.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Claim: every amount from eight rupees upward is a sum of three and five rupee coins.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
The ordinary step cannot work: three rupees is payable and four is not, and six is payable and seven is not.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
So assume the claim for every amount below n, and pay n by paying n minus three and adding a coin.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Strong induction is not stronger: it is the same rule with a wider hypothesis, and any strong proof reformats as an ordinary one.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Well-ordering: every non-empty set of natural numbers has a least element. That is false for the rationals.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
So suppose the claim fails somewhere. The set of counterexamples is non-empty, so it has a least member n.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
But n minus three is below n, so it is payable, so n is payable: a smaller counterexample, which is a contradiction.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Induction, strong induction and well-ordering are three formats for one argument, and any of them reformats as any other.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
A full binary tree is a leaf, or a node with two full binary trees. The definition is already a base and a step.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Claim: with i internal nodes the tree has i plus one leaves. A lone leaf has zero internal nodes and one leaf.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Step: a node over two trees adds one internal node and merges their leaves, so i plus one becomes i plus two.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
The page builds every full binary tree up to six internal nodes: one hundred and ninety-seven trees, and the count holds in all of them.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
A recurrence gives T at n from T at smaller inputs. A closed form gives it in one step, and induction connects the two.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Guess: T of n equals n log base two n. At sixteen that is sixty-four, and at one thousand and twenty-four it is ten thousand two hundred and forty.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Step: T of two k is two T of k plus two k, which by the hypothesis is two k log k plus two k, which is two k log of two k.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
The page computes the recurrence exactly for every power of two up to a million, and the closed form matches at all twenty.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
A loop invariant is a statement true before the loop, after every pass, and at the end: induction with a clock.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Initialization is the base case, maintenance is the step, and termination is the invocation: three obligations, no more.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Insertion sort: after pass i the first i entries are sorted and hold the same values as the original first i entries.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
The page runs insertion sort on all twenty-four orderings of four values and the invariant holds at every index of every run.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
The horse argument: two overlapping groups of n share a middle. With n equal to one the middle is empty.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
A pattern is not a proof: two to the two to the k plus one is prime for k up to four, and composite at k equal to five.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
A step proved only from n equal to two, or only for powers of two, does not cover the claim it is attached to.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
And two different words: inductive reasoning generalises from cases, mathematical induction is a deduction.

